  "query_preview": "..\n\nMoisture associated with the remnants of Isaias is lifting northward\ninto southeast Michigan this morning. The high based cloud cover \nwill transition to thicker lower clouds through the morning and carry\nthrough the bulk of the day. While the deeper gulf moisture holds \noff to the south, rain showers will be supported through the morning \nhours and be focused generally across the southeast half of the CWA. \nRain amounts expected to be light. Overcast skies for most of the \narea will help keep daytime highs today down in the 60s, though parts\nof the Saginaw Valley may make a run up into the low 70s as thicker \nlow clouds may not make it that far north as high clouds pull east \nduring the afternoon. Remnant moisture will pull east throughout the \nafternoon with a period of mid-upper level dry air filtering into \nsoutheast Michigan tonight into Monday. Forecast soundings show a \ndeveloping subsidence inversion tonight with dewpoints lingering in \nthe mid 50s will set the stage for at least some patchy fog \ndevelopment prior to sunrise tomorrow morning. \n\nA low amplitude ridge will take up a short residence time over \nMichigan tomorrow allowing for a dry period through the afternoon. \nPassing of the ridge axis early in the day will lead to increasing \nsouthwest flow and associated warm advection with temperatures at \n850mb climbing to around 15C. This will help daytime highs push into \nthe mid-upper 70s and possibly topping 80 degrees in some locations. \n\nTroughing develops over the Intermountain West Monday and Tuesday \nwith downstream southwest flow increasing atop a frontal zone \nstretching from the central plains into the Midwest. As larger scale \nascent becomes more favorable with help from the increasing LLJ, it \nwill trigger a weak low pressure system along the frontal zone that \nwill move across the western Great Lakes states and drive a cold \nfront through the central Great Lakes on Tuesday. This will bring a \nstretch of increased precipitation chances Monday night through \nTuesday. General thunderstorms chances will exist during this time \nwithin a modestly sheared (~40kts of 0-6km bulk shear) and weakly \nunstable environment (mid-level lapse rates 6-7 C/km) ahead of the \napproaching cold front.\n\nCold air advection will ensue behind the front Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday as 850mb temperatures drop by about 10C pulling \ndaytime highs back down towards mid-October averages. Expect daytime \nhighs in the 60s with overnight lows in the 40s for the late half of \nthe work week. Periodic chances for lake induced showers will be \npossible Wednesday to Thursday morning associate with northerly flow\nthat will also cause higher coverage hanging around as a high \npressure tracks east across the northern Lakes. Canadian troughing \nthen develops late week a should bring a return of widespread \nprecipitation Thursday night into Friday with a much colder airmass \nexpected over the weekend.",
